{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Lemmas","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.diagonal_gram_kills_every_cost_on_its_diagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.diagonal_gram_kills_every_cost_on_its_diagonal","kind":"theorem","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L222-L254","doc":"**A DIAGONAL Gram kills every cost built on its own diagonal.**\n\nThis is the general form of the negative measured in\n`qbeta_ecat_lp_radical_pairing_probe` (5/5), and it is stated for the diagonal\ncase because that is the case measured -- the dense-Gram version needs a sum\nswap this file does not carry, and claiming it would be asserting more than is\nproved.\n\nSetting: `G = diag g`, so `t_j = g_j * u_j` for a cost built from the diagonal,\nand `v` is in the radical, i.e. `g_j * v_j = 0` for every `j`. Then\n`⟨t, v⟩ = 0` — so by §2 the shadow price collapses.\n\n**The block-basis instance.** There `g_λ = y_λ * dim_λ` and the cost is\n`t_λ = y_λ * f(λ)`, which is `g_λ * u_λ` with `u_λ = f(λ)/dim_λ` wherever\n`dim_λ ≠ 0`. So the measured collapse was an instance of this and not a\ncoincidence of convention D: the SAME `y_λ` that makes the Gram entry vanish\nmakes the cost component vanish, for every `f`.\n\n**The design constraint for whoever builds the next cost:** against a diagonal\nGram, no cost proportional to that diagonal escapes. Escaping requires a\ncomponent where the Gram has none — on the six-operator basis, a component\nalong the q-antisymmetriser `(-q³, q², q², -q, -q, 1)`. Being geometric is not\nsufficient and being non-Hecke is not sufficient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.cost_from_gram_makes_objective_a_constraint_readout","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.cost_from_gram_makes_objective_a_constraint_readout","kind":"theorem","line":196,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L196-L220","doc":"**A cost drawn from the Gram makes the objective a read-out of the\nconstraints.**\n\nIf the cost is obtained by pairing each basis vector against a fixed `u`\nthrough the Gram — `tⱼ = ⟨G eⱼ, u⟩` — then\n\n    ⟨t, x⟩ = ⟨G x, u⟩\n\nso the objective reports a linear functional OF THE CONSTRAINT DATA `G x` and\ncarries no information the constraints do not already fix. Taking `u = eₖ`\nrecovers the recorded Hecke defect exactly: `tⱼ = G_{kj}` gives\n`⟨t, x⟩ = (Gx)ₖ`, which is `t·x = u₀` at `k = 0` — constant on a feasible set\nthat pins that coordinate.\n\nRead with §2 this says precisely what a replacement objective must avoid, and\nit is two independent conditions, both checkable before solving: *not drawn\nfrom the Gram it is paired with*, and *not orthogonal to that Gram's radical*.\nBeing merely \"different from the Hecke cost\" is neither. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_is_solver_independent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_is_solver_independent","kind":"theorem","line":180,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L180-L192","doc":"**The price is solver-independent**: any two dual-feasible shadow prices\nagree. So the ratio above is a CHECK the eventual compute must satisfy, not a\nprediction the compute confirms — a solver returning anything else is wrong. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_ne_zero_of_cost_not_orthogonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_ne_zero_of_cost_not_orthogonal","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L163-L178","doc":"**THE ESCAPE.** A cost NOT orthogonal to the radical forces a nonzero\nshadow price.\n\nSo the geometric cost's fate is settled by `⟨t, v⟩` alone. This is the\nstatement that makes running the LP on `t_λ` a decidable question rather than\na hopeful one — and note the direction: this does not say the geometric cost\nclears the bar, only that clearing it is exactly `⟨t, v⟩ ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_zero_of_cost_orthogonal_to_radical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_zero_of_cost_orthogonal_to_radical","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L147-L161","doc":"**The recorded Hecke collapse, as a special case.**\n\nA cost orthogonal to the radical forces `y₀ = 0`. This is\n`y_0_eq_zero_of_radical`'s conclusion reached from the same pairing, and it\nholds with no hypothesis about feasibility, symmetry, or signs -- which is why\nthe collapse cannot be escaped by adjusting the constraints. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_determined_by_radical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.dual_price_determined_by_radical","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L131-L145","doc":"**`y₀ = ⟨t, v⟩ / ⟨1, v⟩`.**\n\nThe shadow price of the net constraint is fixed by two inner products against\nthe radical vector. No feasible point, no `n_target`, no solver, no ordering.\n\nThis is the formalize-first payoff: whether the geometric cost gives a live\ndual is decided by ONE number, `⟨t, v⟩`, and the probe that follows has to\ncompute that and nothing else to know whether the LP is worth running. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.radical_pairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatLPDualRadical.radical_pairing","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatLPDualRadical.lean#L109-L127","doc":"**The radical pairing.**\n\n`Gy` is the image `G y` of the dual variables; `v` spans the radical of `G`, so\n`⟨Gy, v⟩ = ⟨y, Gv⟩ = 0` -- carried here as the hypothesis `hGy`, since it is a\nfact about `G` and `v` and not about the LP. Pairing the dual equality\n`y₀·1 + Gy = t` with `v` leaves one scalar equation.\n\nEverything below is a corollary of this line. ","signatureChunk":0}]}